A||||3||Gokart||Cracked boot||||8||
Z||000000||Gokart||02-27-2000||07:50 PM||||Was looking under my GT today and noticed a line of grease on my oil pan. Not oil, grease! Looked at the boot directly in front of it and it's got a damn crack in it. How serious is this and how soon should I get it fixed? How much do you think it will cost and I know I can't do this job myself so would you recommend, Midas or Meineke? TIA for reply||||reg||8
Z||000001||SCCA FIERO||02-27-2000||08:13 PM||||I would get it done as soon as possible. One of mine ripped (actually 3) and I repaired one of them with those split boots. You don't have to remove the axle to replace it. I did remove the axle for 2 of the 3 and it was not very fun. The one I used the split boot on worked very good and is still fine (after about 4 years). It took me about an hour to put the split one on and about 4 hours to do the noraml kind of boot. The split boot comes with a liquid that melts the boot together after you get in on then you just need to clamp each end. If I can do it anyone can. I got then split type boots at Checker or Autozone so they should be available at just about any parts store.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Gokart||02-28-2000||06:21 AM||||If I decide to repair it myself with a slip boot do I need to load the cracked one with grease seeing how some has come out already? How much if so?||||reg||5
Z||000003||SCCA FIERO||02-28-2000||07:49 AM||||The grease is included with the boot. If it's been torn for a while I would get some brake cleaner and use the complete bottle on the joint to get rid of all the old grease... it should take all the crud with it.||||reg||1
